problems.problem:
Let $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ and $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n$ be $2n$ real numbers. Prove that the following two statements are equivalent:

(i) for any $n$ real numbers $x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n$ satisfying $x_1 \leq x_2 \leq \cdots \leq x_n$ we have $\sum_{k = 1}^{n} a_k x_k \leq \sum_{k = 1}^{n} b_k x_k$;

(ii) $\sum_{k = 1}^{s} a_k \leq \sum_{k = 1}^{s} b_k$ for every $s\in\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}$, and $\sum_{k = 1}^{n} a_k = \sum_{k = 1}^{n} b_k$.

Given a positive integer $A$ with decimal expansion $(a_n,a_{n-1}, \ldots, a_0)$, let $f(A)=\sum_{k=0}^{n} 2^{n-k} a_k$. Define $A_1=f(A)$, $A_2=f(A_1)$, and so on. Prove that

(i) there exists a positive integer $k$ for which $A_{k+1}=A_k$;

(ii) find this $A_k$ for $A=19^{86}$.

In a tetrahedron $ABCD$, points $E$, $F$, $G$ lie on the segments $AB$, $AC$, $AD$, respectively. Prove that

(i) $\operatorname{area}(EFG) \leq \max\{\operatorname{area}(ABC),\operatorname{area}(ABD),\operatorname{area}(ACD),\operatorname{area}(BCD)\}$;

(ii) the same holds with "area" replaced by "perimeter".

Let $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ be real numbers with $n \geq 3$, and let $p$ and $q$ be their elementary symmetric sums of degree $1$ and $2$, respectively. Prove that

(i) $p^2 \cdot \frac{n-1}{n}-2q \geq 0$;

(ii) $\left|x_i - \frac{p}{n}\right| \leq \frac{n-1}{n}\sqrt{p^2 - \frac{2nq}{n-1}}$ for every $i$.

(a) For every positive integer $k$ find the smallest positive integer $f(k)$ such that there exist $5$ sets $s_1,s_2, \ldots , s_5$ satisfying:

(i) each has $k$ elements;

(ii) $s_i$ and $s_{i+1}$ are disjoint for $i=1,2,\ldots,5$ (where $s_6=s_1$);

(iii) the union of the $5$ sets has exactly $f(k)$ elements.

(b) Generalize to $n \geq 3$ sets instead of $5$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfying

(i) $f(x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_{1988}) = f(x_1)f(x_2) \cdots f(x_{1988})$ for all $x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{1988} \in \mathbb{Q}$;

(ii) $\overline{f(1988)}f(x) = f(1988)\overline{f(x)}$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$.

For $n \in \mathbb{N}$, $P(n)$ denotes the number of partitions of $n$ into a sum of positive integers (disregarding order); e.g. $P(4)=5$, since $4 = 1+1+1+1 = 1+1+2 = 1+3 = 2+2 = 4$. The dispersion of a partition is the number of distinct parts in it, and $q(n)$ denotes the sum of the dispersions of all partitions of $n$; e.g. $q(4)=1+2+2+1+1=7$. Prove that for $n \geq 1$

(1) $q(n) = 1 + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} P(i)$;

(2) $1 + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} P(i) \leq \sqrt{2n}\, P(n)$.

On a set $S$ an operation $\circ$ is given which assigns to all $a,b \in S$ an element $a \circ b \in S$, such that

(i) $(a \circ b) \circ c = a \circ (b \circ c)$ for all $a,b,c \in S$;

(ii) $a \circ b \neq b \circ a$ whenever $a \neq b$.

Prove that

(a) $(a \circ b) \circ c = a \circ c$ for all $a,b,c \in S$;

(b) if $S = \{1,2, \ldots, 1990\}$, an operation $\circ$ on $S$ with the above properties can be defined.

Let $M = \{ 2, 3, 4, \ldots, 1000 \}$. Find the smallest $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that any $n$-element subset of $M$ contains three pairwise disjoint $4$-element subsets $S, T, U$ such that

(I) for any two elements of $S$, the larger is a multiple of the smaller, and the same holds for $T$ and for $U$;

(II) $\gcd(s,t) = 1$ for all $s \in S$, $t \in T$;

(III) $\gcd(s,u) > 1$ for all $s \in S$, $u \in U$.

Prove that there exists $m \in \mathbb{N}$ for which there is an integer sequence $(a_n)$ satisfying

(I) $a_0 = 1$, $a_1 = 337$;

(II) $(a_{n + 1} a_{n - 1} - a_n^2) + \frac{3}{4}(a_{n + 1} + a_{n - 1} - 2a_n) = m$ for all $n \geq 1$;

(III) $\frac{1}{6}(a_n + 1)(2a_n + 1)$ is a perfect square for all $n \geq 1$.

Find all polynomials $f(x)$ with real coefficients satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $f(x) = a_0 x^{2n} + a_2 x^{2n - 2} + \cdots + a_{2n - 2} x^2 + a_{2n}$, $a_0 > 0$;

(ii) $\sum_{j=0}^n a_{2j} a_{2n - 2j} \leq \binom{2n}{n} a_0 a_{2n}$;

(iii) all roots of $f(x)$ are purely imaginary.

For a fixed $\theta \in \left(0, \frac{\pi}{2}\right)$, find the smallest $a \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(I) $\frac{\sqrt a}{\cos \theta} + \frac{\sqrt a}{\sin \theta} > 1$;

(II) there exists $x \in \left[ 1 - \frac{\sqrt a}{\sin \theta}, \frac{\sqrt a}{\cos \theta} \right]$ such that $$\left[ (1 - x)\sin \theta - \sqrt{a - x^2 \cos^2 \theta} \right]^2 + \left[ x\cos \theta - \sqrt{a - (1 - x)^2 \sin^2 \theta} \right]^2 \leq a.$$

Let $S = \{ 1, 2, \ldots, 15 \}$, and let $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_n$ be subsets of $S$ satisfying

(I) $|A_i| = 7$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots, n$;

(II) $|A_i \cap A_j| \leq 3$ for $1 \leq i < j \leq n$;

(III) for any $3$-element subset $M$ of $S$ there exists $A_k$ with $M \subset A_k$.

Find the smallest possible value of $n$.

For every permutation $\tau = (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_{10})$ of $1, 2, \ldots, 10$ define $S(\tau) = \sum_{k = 1}^{10} |2x_k - 3x_{k + 1}|$, where $x_{11} = x_1$. Find

(I) the maximum and the minimum values of $S(\tau)$;

(II) the number of $\tau$ for which $S(\tau)$ attains its maximum;

(III) the number of $\tau$ for which $S(\tau)$ attains its minimum.

For an integer $a \geq 2$, let $N_a$ be the number of positive integers $k$ with the following property: the sum of the squares of the digits of $k$ in base $a$ equals $k$. Prove that

(a) $N_a$ is odd;

(b) for every positive integer $M$ there exists an integer $a \geq 2$ with $N_a \geq M$.

Let $n$ be a positive integer and $M = \{(x, y)\mid x, y \text{ integers},\ 1 \leq x, y \leq n\}$. Consider functions $f$ on $M$ with the following properties:

(a) $f(x, y)$ takes non-negative integer values;

(b) $\sum_{y=1}^n f(x, y) = n - 1$ for $1 \leq x \leq n$;

(c) if $f(x_1, y_1)f(x_2, y_2) > 0$, then $(x_1 - x_2)(y_1 - y_2) \geq 0$.

Find $N(n)$, the number of functions $f$ satisfying all the conditions, and give the explicit value of $N(4)$.

Seventeen football fans planned to go to Korea to watch World Cup matches, and selected $17$ matches. Their bookings of admission tickets satisfied the following conditions:

• each person booked at most one ticket for each match;

• the tickets booked by any two persons had at most one match in common;

• one person booked six tickets.

At most how many tickets did the fans book?

Given $n\geq 1$ real numbers $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$, prove that there exist real numbers $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n$ such that

(a) $a_i - b_i$ is a positive integer for every $1 \leq i \leq n$;

(b) $\sum_{1 \leq i < j \leq n} (b_i - b_j)^2 \leq \frac{n^2-1}{12}$.

Let $k\geq 3$ be odd. Prove that there exists an integer-valued polynomial $f$ of degree $k$ with non-integer coefficients with the following properties:

(1) $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1$;

(2) there exist infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that if the equation $n= f(x_1)+\cdots+f(x_s)$ has a solution in integers $x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_s$, then $s \geq 2^k-1$.

Find the maximal constant $M$ such that for every integer $n\geq 3$ there exist two sequences of positive real numbers $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ and $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ satisfying

(1) $\sum_{k = 1}^{n}b_k = 1$ and $2b_k\geq b_{k - 1} + b_{k + 1}$ for $k = 2,3,\ldots,n - 1$;

(2) $a_k^2\leq 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^{k}a_ib_i$ for $k = 1,2,\ldots,n$, and $a_n = M$.

Real numbers $a_i,b_i$ ($i=0,1,\ldots,2n$) satisfy the following conditions:

(1) $a_i+a_{i+1}\geq 0$ for $i=0,1,\ldots,2n-1$;

(2) $a_{2j+1}\leq 0$ for $j=0,1,\ldots,n-1$;

(3) $\sum_{k=2p}^{2q}b_k>0$ for all integers $0\leq p\leq q\leq n$.

Prove that $\sum_{i=0}^{2n}(-1)^i a_i b_i\geq 0$, and determine when equality holds.

Find all positive integers $m,n\geq 2$ such that

(1) $m+1$ is a prime of the form $4k-1$;

(2) there are a prime $p$ and a nonnegative integer $a$ such that $$\frac{m^{2^n-1}-1}{m-1}=m^n+p^a.$$

Let $A$ be a finite set and $A_1,A_2,\ldots, A_n$ subsets of $A$ with the following properties:

(1) $|A_1|=|A_2|=\cdots=|A_n|=k$, where $k>\frac{|A|}{2}$;

(2) for any $a,b\in A$ there exist $A_r,A_s,A_t$ ($1\leq r<s<t\leq n$) with $a,b\in A_r\cap A_s\cap A_t$;

(3) $|A_i\cap A_j|\leq 3$ for all $1\leq i<j\leq n$.

Find all possible values of $n$ when $k$ attains its maximum among all such systems $(A_1,A_2,\ldots, A_n,A)$.

Given an integer $n\geq 2$ and real numbers $x_1,x_2,\ldots, x_n$ in $[0,1]$, prove that there exist real numbers $a_0,a_1,\ldots,a_n$ satisfying

(1) $a_0+a_n=0$;

(2) $|a_i|\leq 1$ for $i=0,1,\ldots,n$;

(3) $|a_i-a_{i-1}|=x_i$ for $i=1,2,\ldots,n$.

Given an integer $n\ge 2$, prove that there exist only finitely many $n$-tuples of positive integers $(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)$ which simultaneously satisfy the following three conditions:

• $a_1>a_2>\ldots>a_n$;

• $\gcd (a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)=1$;

• $a_1=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\gcd (a_i,a_{i+1})$, where $a_{n+1}=a_1$.

There are $n$ balls numbered $1,2,\ldots,n$. They are painted with $4$ colours, red, yellow, blue and green, according to the following rules. First, place them on a circle in an arbitrary order. Then, for any three clockwise consecutive balls numbered $i, j, k$ in this order:

1) if $i>j>k$, the ball $j$ is painted red;

2) if $i<j<k$, the ball $j$ is painted yellow;

3) if $i<j$ and $k<j$, the ball $j$ is painted blue;

4) if $i>j$ and $k>j$, the ball $j$ is painted green.

Thus each arrangement of the balls determines a painting. Two paintings are called distinct if some ball receives different colours in them. Find the number of distinct paintings.

Find all positive real numbers $r<1$ such that there exists a set $\mathcal{S}$ with the following properties:

i) for any real number $t$, exactly one of $t, t+r$ and $t+1$ belongs to $\mathcal{S}$;

ii) for any real number $t$, exactly one of $t, t-r$ and $t-1$ belongs to $\mathcal{S}$.

Prove that there exist a positive constant $K$ and a sequence of integers $(a_n)$ satisfying:

(1) $0<a_1<a_2<\cdots <a_n<\cdots$;

(2) for any positive integer $n$, $a_n<1.01^n K$;

(3) the sum of any finite number of distinct terms of $(a_n)$ is not a perfect square.

Let $S=\{1,2,3,\ldots,2017\}$. For every subset $A$ of $S$ a real number $f(A)\geq 0$ is defined such that:

(1) for any $A,B\subseteq S$, $f(A\cup B)+f(A\cap B)\leq f(A)+f(B)$;

(2) for any $A\subseteq B\subseteq S$, $f(A)\leq f(B)$;

(3) for any $k,j\in S$, $$f(\{1,2,\ldots,k+1\})\geq f(\{1,2,\ldots,k\}\cup \{j\});$$

(4) $f(\varnothing)=0$.

Prove that for any three-element subset $T$ of $S$, the inequality $$f(T)\leq \frac{27}{19}f(\{1,2,3\})$$ holds.

Let $M$ be a subset of $\mathbb{R}$ such that the following conditions are satisfied:

a) for any $x \in M$ and $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, one has $x+n \in M$;

b) for any $x \in M$, one has $-x \in M$;

c) both $M$ and $\mathbb{R}\setminus M$ contain an interval of positive length.

For any real $x$, let $M(x) = \{ n \in \mathbb{Z}^{+} \mid nx \in M \}$. Show that if $\alpha,\beta$ are reals such that $M(\alpha) = M(\beta)$, then one of $\alpha + \beta$ and $\alpha - \beta$ is rational.

Find the number of ordered $100$-tuples $(x_1,\ldots,x_{100})$ satisfying the following conditions:

(i) $x_1,\ldots,x_{100}\in\{1,2,\ldots,2017\}$;

(ii) $2017\mid x_1+\cdots+x_{100}$;

(iii) $2017\mid x_1^2+\cdots+x_{100}^2$.

A graph with $n$ vertices is called $k$-flowing-chromatic if:

1. one can place a chess piece, coloured with one of $k$ colours, on each vertex so that any two pieces on adjacent vertices have different colours;

2. one can choose a Hamiltonian cycle $v_1,v_2,\ldots , v_n$ such that, after moving the piece on $v_i$ to $v_{i+1}$ for all $i=1,2,\ldots ,n$ (with $v_{n+1}=v_1$), any two pieces on adjacent vertices still have different colours;

3. after performing step 2 some number of times, every piece has visited each of the $n$ vertices.

Let $T(G)$ denote the least number $k$ such that $G$ is $k$-flowing-chromatic; if no such $k$ exists, let $T(G)=0$. Let $\chi (G)$ denote the chromatic number of $G$. Find all positive integers $m$ for which there is a graph $G$ with $\chi (G)\le m$ and $T(G)\ge 2^m$ that has no cycle of length less than $2017$.

Let $M,a,b,r$ be non-negative integers with $a,r\ge 2$, and suppose there exists a function $f:\mathbb{Z}\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(1) for all $n\in \mathbb{Z}$, $f^{(r)}(n)=an+b$, where $f^{(r)}$ denotes the composition of $r$ copies of $f$;

(2) for all $n\ge M$, $f(n)\ge 0$;

(3) for all $n>m>M$, $n-m\mid f(n)-f(m)$.

Show that $a$ is a perfect $r$-th power.

Find all positive integers $n$ such that there exist $n$ points $P_1,\ldots,P_n$ on the unit circle with the property that $\sum_{i=1}^n MP_i^k$ takes the same value for every point $M$ on the unit circle, where

a) $k=2018$;

b) $k=2019$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that

1) $f(0,x)$ is non-decreasing in $x$;

2) for any $x,y \in \mathbb{R}$, $f(x,y)=f(y,x)$;

3) for any $x,y,z \in \mathbb{R}$, $(f(x,y)-f(y,z))(f(y,z)-f(z,x))(f(z,x)-f(x,y))=0$;

4) for any $x,y,a \in \mathbb{R}$, $f(x+a,y+a)=f(x,y)+a$.

Let $n\ge2$ be a positive integer. Find the minimum $m$ such that there exist $x_{ij}$ ($1\le i ,j\le n$) satisfying:

(1) for every $1\le i ,j\le n$, $x_{ij}=\max\{x_{i1},x_{i2},\ldots,x_{ij}\}$ or $x_{ij}=\max\{x_{1j},x_{2j},\ldots,x_{ij}\}$;

(2) for every $1\le i \le n$, there are at most $m$ indices $k$ with $x_{ik}=\max\{x_{i1},x_{i2},\ldots,x_{ik}\}$;

(3) for every $1\le j \le n$, there are at most $m$ indices $k$ with $x_{kj}=\max\{x_{1j},x_{2j},\ldots,x_{kj}\}$.

Let $a, b, c, p, q, r$ be positive integers with $p, q, r \ge 2$. Denote $$Q=\{(x, y, z)\in \mathbb{Z}^3 : 0 \le x \le a,\ 0 \le y \le b ,\ 0 \le z \le c \}.$$ Initially, some pieces are placed on the points of $Q$, $M$ pieces in total. Then one can repeatedly perform the following three types of operations:

(1) remove $p$ pieces from $(x, y, z)$ and place a piece on $(x-1, y, z)$;

(2) remove $q$ pieces from $(x, y, z)$ and place a piece on $(x, y-1, z)$;

(3) remove $r$ pieces from $(x, y, z)$ and place a piece on $(x, y, z-1)$.

Find the smallest positive integer $M$ such that, no matter how the pieces are distributed initially, one can always perform a sequence of operations that places a piece on $(0,0,0)$.

Given a positive integer $n \ge 2$, find all $n$-tuples of positive integers $(a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n)$ such that $1<a_1 \le a_2 \le a_3 \le \cdots \le a_n$, $a_1$ is odd, and

(1) $M=\frac{1}{2^n}(a_1-1)a_2 a_3 \cdots a_n$ is a positive integer;

(2) one can pick $n$-tuples of integers $(k_{i,1},k_{i,2},\ldots,k_{i,n})$ for $i=1,2,\ldots,M$ such that for any $1 \le i_1 <i_2 \le M$ there exists $j \in \{1,2,\ldots,n\}$ with $k_{i_1,j}-k_{i_2,j} \not\equiv 0, \pm 1 \pmod{a_j}$.

Initially, each unit square of an $n \times n$ grid is coloured red, yellow or blue. In each round, the following operation is performed for every unit square simultaneously:

• a red square that has a common edge with a yellow square is coloured yellow;

• a yellow square that has a common edge with a blue square is coloured blue;

• a blue square that has a common edge with a red square is coloured red.

It is known that after several rounds all unit squares of the grid have the same colour. Prove that the grid became monochromatic no later than the end of the $(2n-2)$-th round.

In a non-isosceles acute triangle $ABC$ with orthocenter $H$, let $AP$, $BQ$, $CR$ be the altitudes. Let $A_1$ be the midpoint of $BC$; $AA_1$ intersects $QR$ at $K$, and $QR$ intersects the line through $A$ parallel to $BC$ at $D$. The line connecting $K$ and the midpoint of $AH$ intersects $DA_1$ at $A_2$. Define $B_2$, $C_2$ similarly. Triangle $A_2B_2C_2$ is known to be non-degenerate; let $\omega$ be its circumcircle. Prove that there are circles $\odot A'$, $\odot B'$, $\odot C'$, internally tangent to $\omega$, such that:

(1) $\odot A'$ is tangent to $AB$ and $AC$, $\odot B'$ is tangent to $BC$ and $BA$, and $\odot C'$ is tangent to $CA$ and $CB$;

(2) $A'$, $B'$, $C'$ are distinct and collinear.

Find the largest positive integer $m$ for which it is possible to colour some cells of a $70\times 70$ table red such that

• there are no two red cells such that the two rows containing them have the same number of red cells and the two columns containing them also have the same number of red cells;

• there are two rows with exactly $m$ red cells each.

Prove that there exists a positive real number $\lambda$ such that for any real number $D>1$ one can find an acute triangle $ABC$ in the Cartesian plane such that

• $A, B, C$ are lattice points;

• $AB, BC, CA>D$;

• the area of triangle $ABC$ is less than $\frac{\sqrt 3}{4}D^2+\lambda\cdot D^{4/5}$.

Do there exist pairwise distinct integers $a_1, a_2, \ldots$ satisfying:

(1) $a_{k^2}>0$ and $a_{k^2+k}<0$ for all positive integers $k$;

(2) $\left| a_{n+1}-a_n\right|\leq 2023\sqrt n$ for all positive integers $n$?

Let $P(x), Q(x)$ be non-constant real polynomials such that for every positive integer $m$ there exists a positive integer $n$ with $P(m) = Q(n)$. Prove that:

(1) if $\deg Q \mid \deg P$, then there exists a real polynomial $h(x)$ such that $P(x) = Q(h(x))$ for all real numbers $x$;

(2) $\deg Q \mid \deg P$.

Prove that there exist integers $x_1,x_2,\ldots, x_{10},y_1,y_2,\ldots, y_{10}$ satisfying the following conditions:

(1) $|x_i|,|y_i|\le 10^{10}$ for all $1\le i \le 10$;

(2) the set $$S = \left\{ \left( \sum_{i=1}^{10} a_i x_i, \sum_{i=1}^{10} a_i y_i \right) : a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_{10} \in \{0, 1\} \right\}$$ has $|S| = 1024$, and any strip of width $1$ (the region between two parallel lines at distance $1$) covers at most two points of $S$.
